<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    examples/attacker-app/AndroidManifest.xml

    The attacker in labs 01 and 04. It is deliberately as boring as possible:
    ONE launcher activity, NO permissions, NO shared uid, NO intent filters
    beyond the launcher entry.

    That emptiness is the point of the whole exercise. The exploit needs no
    permission the user has to grant, no root, no accessibility service, and no
    special capability. It is an ordinary app — the kind that gets installed for
    a wallpaper or a torch — and the entire attack is fifteen lines of Java.

    IT IS ALSO WHY YOU CANNOT DO THIS WITH `adb shell`. Shell runs as uid 2000
    and is privileged; this app runs as an ordinary 10xxx uid, which is what the
    finding must be proved against. See README section 7.6.

    <queries> is declared because from targetSdk 30 an app cannot see, or resolve
    against, packages it has not declared an interest in. Without it the attacker's
    own resolveActivity() calls would return null against the deputy — which is a
    good illustration of package-visibility filtering in its own right, and lab 01
    has you comment it out to watch that happen.

    AUTHORISED TESTING ONLY.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="com.pentest.attacker">

    <uses-sdk android:minSdkVersion="24" android:targetSdkVersion="30" />

    <!-- Package visibility, API 30+. Remove it and watch resolveActivity() fail. -->
    <queries>
        <package android:name="com.pentest.deputy" />
    </queries>

    <application
        android:label="Photo Filters"
        android:allowBackup="false">

        <activity android:name=".ExploitActivity" android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

    </application>
</manifest>
